23:03:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: In most first-person shooters, the bullet you see flying across the map is only a *visual effect* for your local client. The actual logic that decides whether you hit or miss a target is handled by a combination of **instant raycasting** (hitscan) or a **simulated physic object** (projectile), and when you play online, a whole layer of network tricks makes it feel instant and fair. Here’s a breakdown of how it works, step by step. --- ### 1. Bullet Trajectory on Your Machine There are two main ways the game “creates” the bullet path locally: **A. Hitscan (instant hit)** - When you pull the trigger, the game engine fires an invisible ray from the centre of your screen/crosshair into the game world. - It instantly checks what the ray hits – a wall, a player, an object. If it hits an enemy, damage is applied immediately (subject to server confirmation). - Because there’s no travel time, the visual tracer you see (the glowing streak) is just a cosmetic effect drawn to make the shot feel powerful. - Most weapons in games like *Call of Duty*, *Valorant*, and shotguns (with multiple rays) use hitscan. **B. Projectile Simulation (ballistic bullets)** - The game spawns a tiny, invisible object with mass, speed, and often affected by gravity or drag. - Each frame (up to hundreds of times per second), the engine moves this object along its velocity vector and checks for collisions with the game world (usually via raycasting or sweep tests between frames). - If it hits a player or surface, the effect is triggered. Because this takes time, you have to lead your target, and the bullet can drop over distance. - Games like *Battlefield*, *Apex Legends*, and sniper rifles in many titles use projectiles for realism. In both cases, **the bullet you see on your screen (the tracer) is just a visual effect** – the actual collision is calculated by the game engine behind the scenes. --- ### 2. What Happens Online – Making It Work in Real Time The real magic is how this works over the internet with latency (ping). You press the trigger, the data travels to the server, and the server tells everyone what happened – all in milliseconds. Here’s how it stays responsive: **A. Client-side prediction** - The moment you fire, your game *predicts* the outcome locally. It shows the muzzle flash, recoil, tracer, and even blood effects right away, without waiting for the server. - This makes shooting feel instantaneous, even if your ping is 50 ms. If you hit an enemy on your screen, you’ll often hear the hit marker sound immediately – but the official “kill” isn’t confirmed until the server agrees. **B. Server authority & validation** - The server is the ultimate referee. When you fire, a message is sent: “Player A fired weapon X at time T, looking in direction D.” - The server then replays the shot in its own authoritative game state. For hitscan, it does a raycast on its version of the world. For a projectile, it might simulate the bullet from the moment you fired, taking into account the exact server time. - If the server agrees you hit an opponent, it broadcasts that damage to all clients. If your prediction was wrong (maybe due to lag or a cheater), the server will correct your client (e.g., the enemy might suddenly snap back to a position or you see your shots miss after all). **C. Lag compensation – the “rewind” trick** - To avoid having to lead your target to compensate for latency, most modern FPS games use **lag compensation**. - The server stores a short history (a few hundred milliseconds) of every player’s hitbox positions. - When it receives your “fire” command, the server looks at the timestamp of when *you* fired on your screen. It rewinds the enemy hitboxes back to what they were at that exact moment on your client. - Then it does the raycast/projectile check against those past positions. If a hit is detected, you get the kill, even though the enemy may already have moved on the server. - This is why you can get shot after you’ve ducked behind a wall – on the shooter’s screen (and at their timestamp) you were still visible. The server accepts that reality. **D. Interpolation & player movement** - To show other players smoothly moving on your screen, the server sends their positions at a fixed rate (e.g., 60 ticks per second). Your client **interpolates** between the last two received positions, slightly delaying the displayed position (by about 100 ms) to fill in the gaps. - This means you always see a slightly “old” version of other players, but it looks fluid. Combined with lag compensation, it makes it so you can aim directly at what you see and still get hits. --- ### 3. Visual Feedback vs. Actual Physics It’s important to separate **what you see** from **what the game logic uses**: - **Tracers** (glowing streaks), **muzzle flashes**, **sparks**, and even **blood spatter** on your screen are all purely cosmetic and local. They’re triggered instantly by your client’s prediction. - The real bullet that decides damage is either the invisible ray or the simulated projectile, handled first by your local prediction and then verified by the server. - In projectile-based games, you might actually see the tracers fly but they are still an approximation; the authoritative collision is still done on the server. Some games will even send the exact trajectory path to the server for it to verify. --- ### In Short Your game fires an **invisible ray or a simulated bullet** locally, shows you instant cosmetic feedback, and sends the shot data to the server. The server then **replays the shot** using its authoritative world, often rewinding player positions to your timestamp (lag compensation) to make aiming fair. The combination of **client prediction, server authority, and lag compensation** is what allows you to line up a crosshair on a moving enemy hundreds of miles away and have the bullet connect in real time.
