a tiny distance forward each frame to see if it hit something. - **Sweep tests** that move the bullet’s collision shape between frames to avoid “tunneling” through thin objects at high speeds. - If it hits a player, a wall, or the ground, the bullet stops, deals damage, and triggers visual effects (sparks, blood, etc.). All of this happens **in real time** — the bullet exists in the game world and can be interacted with (though for performance, it’s usually purely visual and not a full physics body you can bump into). --- ### 3. How This Works Across the Network (Online Multiplayer) Now the trick is keeping this bullet trajectory synced across all players. Here’s how: **A. The server is the referee** - The server runs the **authoritative simulation** of the bullet. It spawns the projectile and steps it forward each server tick (e.g., 60 times per second), checking for hits just like the client does. - The server sends a message to all players when the bullet is created: “Bullet type X spawned at position P, direction D, time T.” This allows all clients to recreate an identical bullet in their own simulation. - Because the update rate is high and the physics are deterministic (same gravity, same initial conditions), the bullet on your screen and the bullet on another player’s screen will follow the exact same path, with only tiny, imperceptible differences. **B. Clients run their own bullet simulation locally (prediction)** - Your client doesn’t wait for the server to tell you where the bullet is; it starts simulating immediately. That’s why you see the tracer fly instantly. - The other clients also simulate the same bullet in sync, so everyone sees the bullet travel in real time. - If a hit is detected on a client (like seeing the bullet hit an enemy), the client might show the impact effects right away to feel responsive (prediction), but the server has the final say. **C. The server confirms hits** - When the server’s own bullet simulation detects a collision with a player, it sends out a “damage event” to all clients: “Player A’s bullet hit Player B, dealing X damage.” - Clients that already predicted the hit just adjust if necessary. If your prediction was wrong (e.g., because of a lag spike that caused a mismatch), the server overrules it and corrects the outcome (the enemy might not actually take damage). --- ### 4. Visual Tracers vs. The Real Bullet Even in projectile-based games, what you *see* — the bright glowing streak or smoke trail — is often a separate, purely cosmetic effect. That visual tracer is drawn in real time to follow the bullet’s path, but the actual collision detection is done by the invisible physics projectile. So yes, the trajectory is tracked in two parallel ways: - A **collision body** (invisible) for game logic. - A **particle effect** (visible) for immersion. Both are updated every frame to match the bullet’s real-time motion. --- ### 5. Why the Confusion Happens Many popular shooters (like *Call of Duty*) use **hitscan** for most weapons, so there is no physical bullet at all. The “trajectory” is simply a straight line from your gun to whatever the crosshair points at, and it’s all resolved in a single instant — no real-time tracking. That’s probably why my earlier answer emphasized that the bullet is “just a visual effect” in those cases. But for any game with **projectile physics** (snipers in *Battlefield*, arrows in *Overwatch*, bullets in *Apex Legends*, rockets in *Quake*), you are spot on: the game really does track the bullet as a moving object in real time. The server and all clients simulate it independently but in sync, and the networking tricks (prediction, lag compensation) make it appear seamless. So, in summary: **Yes, in many games, bullets are physically simulated and tracked in real time** — you’re absolutely right. The method I described earlier (instant raycast) is just the other common way, and that can be confusing when both are called “shooting.”
