he answer to “how does the game track my interaction with online players in real time?” — which was your very first question. They ensure that when you pull the trigger, the server knows exactly what you were aiming at on your screen at that moment, and decides hit or miss based on that. --- ### So Where Does the Bullet Itself Fit In? Now, let’s overlay the bullet’s behavior. There are still two distinct methods: #### 1. Hitscan (Instant Ray + Cosmetic Tracer) - The bullet’s path **does not exist as a tracked object**. - When you fire, a single raycast is performed. The sophisticated algorithms (lag compensation, etc.) make sure the server checks your aim against the opponent’s past position. - After the hit/miss is determined, the game draws a **cosmetic tracer** — a visual effect that flies from your gun to the impact point. This tracer is not used for collision; it’s a post-hoc animation. But it *looks* like real-time tracking because it spans several frames and is perfectly synchronized with the muzzle flash and impact sound. - So the *bullet’s flight* is an illusion driven by a cosmetic system, while the *hit accuracy* is driven by the sophisticated networking algorithms. #### 2. Projectile Simulation (Real Physics Object) - Here, the bullet **is a real, simulated object**. - The game spawns a tiny physics projectile that moves every frame according to velocity, gravity, and sometimes drag. - On your client, it’s simulated instantly (prediction). On the server, it’s simulated authoritatively. The two simulations must stay in sync using algorithms like deterministic physics and periodic corrections. - In this case, you genuinely track the bullet’s trajectory in real time, and that trajectory *determines* the hit once it collides. Both methods can (and do) use the same advanced networking algorithms (interpolation, lag compensation) for the players, but the bullet itself is either an instant line or a moving object. --- ### Why the Confusion is Totally Understandable Many modern games **blur the line**. For example: - **Overwatch**: Soldier: 76’s pulse rifle is hitscan, but the visual tracer is so fast it looks like a projectile. His Helix Rockets are actual projectiles. - **Apex Legends**: Every gun is a projectile, even peacekeeper shots (which are actual moving pellets). So yes, the game tracks every single bullet in real time; that’s why you must lead targets. - **Battlefield**: Sniper rifles are projectiles with bullet drop and travel time, while many automatic guns are hitscan with cosmetic tracers (though recent Battlefields have moved to more projectile systems). - **Call of Duty**: Most normal guns are hitscan; rockets and some weapons are projectiles. Warzone introduced projectile ballistics for some long-range guns. If you mainly play games like Apex, PUBG, or Battlefield snipers, then your experience of bullets traveling in real time is **100% genuine** — those are physically tracked projectiles, and the algorithms are doing full simulation. But if you play Valorant or CS:GO, every bullet is hitscan. The tracers you see are visual effects, but the game’s accuracy still relies on those sophisticated tracking algorithms for player positions and hit registration. --- ### So, Were My Earlier Answers Off? I stressed the “visual effect” side because your initial question asked how the game tracks the *trajectory*, and in many FPS titles, the trajectory is *not* a simulated object for most weapons. However, I may have underemphasized that: - The visual tracer *itself* is a carefully crafted real-time effect that precisely follows the instantaneous ray path, so it looks like tracking. - The networking algorithms that make the instant ray accurate *are* tracking real-time data (player positions, timestamps) in a sophisticated way. I didn’t invent anything — hitscan is the standard. But you’re right: the word “tracking” can also refer to the network prediction algorithms, and those are undeniably sophisticated and run in real time. Your suspicion that there’s more to it is spot on. --- ### Bottom Line - **If you play a game where you must lead targets and compensate for drop**, the game is literally tracking the bullet with physics algorithms in real time. - **If you play a game where crosshair placement is instant hit**, the bullet’s flight is a cosmetic trick — but the networking behind that split-second hit involves extremely precise tracking of time, positions, and views to make it fair. Both are correct in different contexts, and both feel like real-time tracking because the visual, audio, and network systems are designed to create that seamless feedback. Your original instinct that games use sophisticated tracking algorithms is right — it’s just that sometimes the “bullet” itself is a simulation, and sometimes it’s a visual consequence of a much more complex web of real-time data processing.
